course: dictionary
lesson: 4759
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. House is a good example. Most of the time it means a building where a family lives. «At my house we have a big garden.» But a house can also be the people in it: «The whole house woke up» means everyone in the home woke up, not that the building did.
explain: Context tells you which meaning to pick. Ask yourself: which meaning fits best here? In some schools, a house is a team that pupils belong to. House can also be a verb, which means to give a place to live or keep: «The museum houses old toys.»
explain: Check your choice by swapping in a meaning and reading the sentence again. If it still makes sense, you have found the right one. If it sounds silly, try another meaning.
fact: House can be a noun or a verb.
fact: As a noun, a house is a building where a family lives.
fact: As a noun, house can also mean the people in the home, as in «The whole house woke up».
fact: As a verb, to house means to give a place to live or keep: «The museum houses old toys».
fact: In some schools, a house is a team that pupils belong to, and pupils can win house points for it.
exercise: «The whole house woke up when the alarm rang.» In this sentence, what does house mean? | The roof and the walls | * The people living in the home | A team in school | To give a place to live
exercise: «The museum houses old toys.» What does houses mean here? | A team at school | A family's home | Free of charge | * To give a place to keep things
exercise: «At my house we have a big garden.» What does house mean? | * A building where a family lives | Something given for free | To keep or give a place to live | A team that pupils belong to
test: «Is your house always like this?» What does house mean here? | Free of charge | A school team | * Your home, the place and the way it is lived in | To give a place to live
test: «A smart meter shows how much energy the house is using right now.» What does house mean here? | A free drink | A team that pupils belong to | * A building where a family lives | To keep old things safe
exam: Which sentence uses house as a verb, meaning to give a place to keep things? | At my house we have a dog | My house is blue | The whole house woke up | * The museum houses old toys
